## Test Anti-Pattern Audit — `ProductCatalogTests.cs`

This is a textbook **"coverage theater"** suite. Every production method is called (→ 95%+ line coverage), but almost nothing is actually **verified**. Here's the severity-ranked breakdown:

---

### 🔴 Critical — No assertions at all (pure "exercise" tests)

| # | Test | Problem | What can break silently |
|---|------|---------|------------------------|
| 1 | `AddProduct_Works` (L17-21) | Calls `AddProduct` then stops. **Zero assertions.** | `Save` could silently drop data; you'd never know. |
| 2 | `UpdatePrice_Works` (L33-36) | Calls `UpdatePrice`, never checks the stored price changed. | Price update could be a no-op, or save the *old* price, or save to the wrong SKU. |
| 3 | `RemoveProduct_Works` (L39-43) | Calls `RemoveProduct`, never checks the product is gone. | Delete could silently fail; product remains in the store. |
| 4 | `GetProductCount_Works` (L63-66) | Calls `GetProductCount` on an **empty** catalog and **discards the result**. | Count could return -1 or `int.MaxValue`; test still passes. |
| 5 | `ApplyBulkDiscount_Works` (L69-74) | Applies a 10% discount, never checks any price changed. | Discount math could be inverted (multiply instead of subtract), apply twice, or corrupt data. |
| 6 | `ImportFromCsv_Works` (L85-88) | Imports one row, never checks the product was actually created. | Parsing bugs, swapped fields, or silent exceptions all pass. |

**Fix pattern** — add outcome assertions:
```csharp
// Example: UpdatePrice_Works
_catalog.AddProduct(new Product("SKU-001", "Widget", 9.99m));
_catalog.UpdatePrice("SKU-001", 14.99m);
var updated = _catalog.GetProduct("SKU-001");
Assert.AreEqual(14.99m, updated!.Price);

// Example: RemoveProduct_Works
_catalog.AddProduct(new Product("SKU-001", "Widget", 9.99m));
_catalog.RemoveProduct("SKU-001");
Assert.IsNull(_catalog.GetProduct("SKU-001"));

// Example: ApplyBulkDiscount_Works
_catalog.AddProduct(new Product("SKU-001", "Widget", 100.00m));
_catalog.ApplyBulkDiscount(0.10m);
Assert.AreEqual(90.00m, _catalog.GetProduct("SKU-001")!.Price);

// Example: GetProductCount_Works
_catalog.AddProduct(new Product("SKU-001", "Widget", 9.99m));
Assert.AreEqual(1, _catalog.GetProductCount());
```

---

### 🟠 High — Weak assertions (`IsNotNull` only)

| # | Test | Problem |
|---|------|---------|
| 7 | `GetProduct_Works` (L25-29) | Asserts `IsNotNull` but never checks `Sku`, `Name`, or `Price`. A store returning `new Product("","",0)` for every lookup passes. |
| 8 | `SearchProducts_Works` (L47-52) | Asserts the list `IsNotNull` but never checks `Count`, contents, or that "Gadget" was excluded. An empty list passes. A list containing *both* products also passes. |
| 9 | `ExportToCsv_Works` (L77-82) | Asserts the CSV string `IsNotNull` but never checks format. An empty string, garbage, or wrong delimiters all pass. |
| 10 | `GetAllProducts_Works` (L55-60) | Same — `IsNotNull` on a list tells you nothing about its count or contents. |

**Fix pattern** — assert on values, not just existence:
```csharp
// Example: GetProduct_Works
var result = _catalog.GetProduct("SKU-001");
Assert.IsNotNull(result);
Assert.AreEqual("SKU-001", result.Sku);
Assert.AreEqual("Widget", result.Name);
Assert.AreEqual(9.99m, result.Price);

// Example: SearchProducts_Works
var results = _catalog.Search("Widget");
Assert.AreEqual(1, results.Count);
Assert.AreEqual("SKU-001", results[0].Sku);

// Example: ExportToCsv_Works
var csv = _catalog.ExportToCsv();
Assert.AreEqual("SKU-001,Widget,9.99", csv);
```

